Every order at Panela is the end of a short journey. Land on the site. Look at the menu. Choose a box. Go to checkout. Pay. {{journey}} At each step some people stop. Count how many reach each step and you have a funnel, wide at the top and narrow at the bottom. {{funnel}} The obvious way to read it is to look for the biggest drop. At Panela that is between the menu and choosing a box, where nearly half of the sessions stop. {{biggest}} It is also, probably, the least interesting drop. People browse menus without wanting to cook this week. That is what menus are for. {{browse}} The interesting drop is the one that is different from what it should be. Split the funnel by device and one step jumps out: at checkout, phones lose half their sessions, and laptops lose one in six. {{device}} That is not browsing. That is a form that does not work on a small screen. {{form}} This lesson builds Panela's funnel, finds the step that is broken rather than merely steep, and sizes what fixing it is worth. {{end}}
